==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*haitaną||command, name}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*kei-}}, {{term/t|ine-pro|*ki-||put in motion, be moving}}. Cognate with Old Frisian {{term|hēta|lang=ofs}}, Old Saxon {{term|hētan|lang=osx}}, Old Norse {{term|heita|lang=non}} (Swedish {{term|heta|lang=sv}}), Gothic {{term|𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽|tr=haitan|lang=got}}. The Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek {{term|κίειν||tr=kíein|put in motion|lang=grc}}, Latin {{term|ciere||rouse, make active|lang=la}} and Albanian {{term|cys||to spur, set in motion|lang=sq}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈhɑːtɑn/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|type=strong|class=VII|head=hātan}}

# to [[order]], [[command]]
# to [[call]] or [[name]]
# {{context|intransitive|lang=ang}} to be called or named

